\n\nAfter calibration, check the result on a second reference point. Warm the widget by holding it in a closed hand for two minutes, then compare the reading with a thermometer in a bag beside it. The difference should be under half a degree. If it is larger, repeat the procedure with a fresh reference reading, because a reference that was still warming up is the most common cause of a poor offset. Note the date, the reference used and the final offset in the device logbook so that the history of each widget can be reviewed. \n\nSensors drift slowly over several years. A widget that was accurate when installed may read half a degree high after three years, which is inside the stated tolerance but can still matter for cold chain records. Schedule a check every twelve months for devices that hold regulated stock, and recalibrate any widget that falls outside the tolerance printed on its label. Devices that are replaced should be calibrated before they join the hub, so that the new serial number starts with a valid offset."}]}

